 "notes": [
  "Units: 1954 ranked segments (4933 km of the BAV graph's 5866 km): the track between two consecutive served stations (or a station and a junction) with trains in the twin's timetable, plus any segment a training incident touched.",
  "Incidents: 543 VIA incidents with an active version (7 withdrawn ones left out); 531 placed on the network (458 sections, 9 cross-border sections by their Swiss part, 64 at one station), 12 not placed (stations abroad). Training 391 (2024-03..2025-12), test 140 (2026-01-01..2026-10-12, the export date); 0.0% of the test weight fell on segments outside the ranked set and counts as a miss for every ranking.",
  "Each incident counts once, spread evenly over the segments of its section (a one-station incident over the segments at that station); the top sets are by number of segments, the km rows show how much track each is.",
  "Model: Poisson regression of the training counts on log km (0.66), log(1 + trains per day) (0.15), single-track share (-0.62) and log past incidents within ~20 km, leaving the segment itself out (1.06), then empirical-Bayes shrinkage of each segment's own count (gamma shape 0.30). Bandwidth and shrinkage chosen on 2024-03..12 -> 2025; rates only from the training period.",
  "Intervals: paired bootstrap over test incidents (2000 resamples); '_weekblock' resamples whole ISO weeks (storms that hit several segments at once).",
  "Exposure is the twin's November 2026 timetable (a Monday; the busiest other day for lines without trains that Monday) applied to the whole period; a train passing without stopping counts too.",
  "The liveticker holds about 200 published, service-interrupting incidents a year, not every SBB disruption, and 70% of them were first written in French (western Switzerland): the map ranks where such published incidents happen; exposure explains little of it, the place's own history explains most.",
  "Not better than: raw training counts. Said plainly: the raw count of past incidents per segment is as good a ranking as the model at these cut-offs."
